  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nGenerally speaking, the synoptic pattern consist of broad ridging \nacross much of the CONUS with a weaker trough over the northeast. A \nstationary boundary remains situated along our southern CWA border, \nand will be the focus for some scattered showers and perhaps a few \nrumbles of thunder this afternoon. Due to the morning overcast \nconditions CAPE is more limited today, thus, strong convection \nthreat is very low. Isolated flooding cannot be ruled out where \npoor antecedent conditions exist, but with the primary axis of \nconvergence to our south and improved storm motion, the flooding \nthreat is also much lower than previous days. \n\nShortwave energy will dive into the Great Lakes region tomorrow, \nwith increasing chances for precipitation as a weak vort max rounds \nthe base of the aforementioned trough, which will then be digging \ninto the southern Apps. CAPE looks to be very limited so this will \nbe predominately showers. Still some disagreement with CAMs on how \nexactly timing/coverage plays out, but trends lean towards a period \nof increased PoP chances Saturday morning into the afternoon. A \nbrief lull will then be possible, with some additional PoP \nchances returning Sat night. \n\nThe trough axis looks to begin a slow eastward shift Sunday, with \nincreasing H5 heights promoting a slight warming trend. Isolated \nconvection cannot be ruled out in vicinity of higher terrain and \nsouthern valley Sunday afternoon, but most will remain dry for the \nbulk of the afternoon and evening. A drier vertical column with high \nDCAPE near 1000 J/kg and mid-level lapse rates between 6.5-7degC \ncould lead to gusty winds if storms can initiate. \n\nAdditional periods of showers and storms will be possible into early \nnext week as bouts of energy round the base of the trough. Highest \nPoP chances will be in north and eastern areas. With enhanced shear \ndue to marginal mid and upper level flow, periods of strong to \nsevere storm chances cannot be ruled out depending on timing and \navailability of instability. Additionally, Monday and Tuesday look \nto be the warmest days with heat indices in the mid 90s to low 100s. \nA slight moderation in temperatures is expected late week.",
